    "chain": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "thought": "Read the paper to understand repository context and whether executing the pad tool is required.",
        "action": "read_paper()",
        "observation": "Contents of /Users/joaovictor/research-agents/Group-25-Research-Project/papers/metapointfinder/paper.pdf:\n1 \n \nMetaPointFinder: Detection of Antimicrobial Resistance -1 \nConferring Point Mutations (ARMs) from Metagenomic Reads  2 \nAldert L. Zomer1,2, Sam Nooij1, Cailean Carter3,4, Alison E. Mather3,4,5† 3 \n1 Department of Biomolecular Health Sciences, Faculty of Veterinary Medicine, Utrecht 4 \nUniversity, the Netherlands  5 \n2 WHO Collaborating Centre for Reference and Research on Campylobacter and Antimicrobial 6 \nResistance from a One Health Perspective / WOAH Reference Laboratory for 7 \nCampylobacteriosis, Utrecht, the Netherlands 8 \n3Quadram Institute Bioscience, Norwich Research Park, Norwich, NR4 7UQ, UK 9 \n4Centre for Microbial Interactions, Norwich Research Park, Norwich, NR4 7UG, UK 10 \n5University of East Anglia, Norwich Research Park, Norwich, NR4 7TJ, UK 11 \n†Corresponding author: [alison.mather@quadram.ac.uk] 12 \nAbstract 13 \nBackground: Many clinically important antimicrobial resistance (AMR) phenotypes such as 14 \nfluoroquinolones and rifamycins are driven by antimicrobial resistance -conferring mutations 15 \n(ARMs) in conserved chromosomal loci (e.g., gyrA, parC, rpoB). Resistome profiling by 16 \nmetagenomics sequencing is often proposed as an ideal AMR surveillance tool as it is organism -17 \nagnostic, but currently, existing metagenomic AMR surveillance pipelines are only able to identify 18 \nacquired AMR genes but not point mutations associated with AMR. This is a serious gap in 19 \nmetagenomics-based AMR surveillance, as the true extent of AMR may be underestimated.  20 \nMethods: We developed MetaPointFinder (v1), a read-based method that can process both long 21 \nand short metagenomic reads. The tool identifies resistance -determining regions in reads using 22 \nDIAMOND (translated protein) and KMA (nucleotide), and classifies known resistant versus wild-23 \ntype variants by aligning the sequences using pwalign and assessing the observed mutations 24 \nbased on known resistance mutations in the AMRFinderPlus database. The tool outputs ARMs per 25 \nread, per gene and per antibiotic class.  26 \nResults: In proof -of-concept analyses, MetaPointFinder identified known AMR -associated 27 \nmutations and quantified resistant/susceptible read counts and ratios from metagenomic 28 \nsamples with corroborating phenotypic resistance data when available. We benchmark our tool 29 \nusing simulated reads from DNA and from reverse-translated protein references with read lengths 30 \nof 100–5000 bp and error rates between 0 and 30%, simulating both Illumina and Nanopore error 31 \nrates. We show that MetaPointFinder outperforms any available method for detection of ARMs 32 \nin metagenomics data.  33 \nConclusion: MetaPointFinder complements gene -centric resistome profiling by capturing 34 \nchromosomal mutation-based AMR directly from metagenomes. 35 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n2 \n \nImpact Statement 36 \nMany clinically important antimicrobial resistance (AMR) phenotypes such as fluoroquinolones 37 \nand rifamycins are driven by antimicrobial resistance -conferring mutations (ARMs) in conserved 38 \nchromosomal loci (e.g., gyrA, parC, rpoB). Resistome profiling by metagenomic sequencing is ideal 39 \nfor AMR surveillance, but existing pipelines detect only acquired genes and ignore point 40 \nmutations. MetaPointFinder bridges this gap.  The tool accurately identifies reads carrying 41 \nresistance-determining regions using DIAMOND (protein) and KMA (DNA), aligns them, and 42 \nclassifies resistant versus wild -type variants using known mutations from the AMRFinderPlus 43 \ndatabase. 44 \nIntroduction 45 \nAntimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a threat to human and animal health globally, and the rapid and 46 \naccurate quantification of AMR is critical to any strategy to reduce its burden. Generation and 47 \navailability of bacterial sequence data and lower costs compared to phenotypic antibiotic 48 \nsusceptibility testing makes genotypic prediction of AMR increasingly common. Many tools exist 49 \nthat facilitate the prediction of AMR from genome sequences such as ResFinder [1], 50 \nAMRFinderPlus [2] or RGI [3]. Most available tools predict resistance from both acquired 51 \nantimicrobial resistance genes (ARGs) , and from antimicrobial resistance mutations (ARMs) in 52 \nresistance-determining regions (RDRs) which are often found in essential genes. The identification 53 \nof genotypic resistance does not necessarily equate to phenotypic resistance, but concordance 54 \nbetween phenotype and genotype is generally fairly high [4–7]. Whole genome -based AMR 55 \nsurveillance requires culturing of isolates  and this restricts the assessment of AMR to specific 56 \ntargeted bacteria. An organism -agnostic alternative which does not require culturing is 57 \nmetagenomic sequencing, where estimation of the resistome can be obtained either by mapping 58 \nreads to ARG databases or by assembling metagenomic reads into contigs and looking for ARGs 59 \nin the assemblies [8–10]. 60 \nMetagenomic sequencing has transformed ARG profiling, as it offers a more complete overview 61 \nof the types and diversity of ARGs within samples. It has been applied in a variety of different 62 \nsettings across the One Health continuum, including surveillance of AMR in wastewater [11, 12], 63 \nfood [13, 14], humans [15, 16] and animals [17]. Typically, the methods used only evaluate the 64 \nabundance of ARGs and are not able to identify ARMs because identification of single nucleotide 65 \npolymorphisms ( SNPs) in metagenomic data is not trivial  because it is difficult to validate 66 \nperformance. The tool Mumame, developed to fill this gap, identifies mutations but displayed  67 \nhighly variable quantitative estimates, even at high sequencing depths [18]. This is a serious gap 68 \nin metagenomics -based AMR surveillance, as the total AMR of a sample is likely to be 69 \nunderestimated if ARMs cannot be reliably identified . ARMs underpin resistance to several 70 \nfrontline classes of antibiotics  such as fluoroquinolones, rifamycins, and folate -pathway 71 \ninhibitors, yet remain undetected in standard AMR pipelines. Although tools such as ResFinder 72 \nand AMRFinderPlus, implemented in many whole genome sequencing and AMR surveillance 73 \npipelines, are highly successful for isolate genome assemblies, they cannot be run in a species -74 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n3 \n \nagnostic manner, necessary for metagenomic samples where multiple bacterial species are 75 \npresent and where minor variant mutations are lost in non -haplotype aware assemblies. There 76 \nremains a practical gap for robust, read -based identification and quantification of known 77 \nresistance-conferring substitutions directly from metagenomes. 78 \nWe present MetaPointFinder (v1), a species -agnostic, read-based approach that detects known 79 \nSNPs and amino-acid substitutions in resistance-determining regions (RDRs) using translated and 80 \nnucleotide alignment and reports resistant/susceptible/unknown read counts. We show the 81 \ndetection of substitutions in the quinolone resistance-determining region of DNA gyrase subunit 82 \nA gene GyrA (e.g., S83L, D87N), where association with fluoroquinolone resistance is well 83 \nestablished and compare with available phenotypic data, we show the detection of SNPs and 84 \namino-acid substitutions and evaluate ARM counts in various public datasets. Finally, we also 85 \nevaluate Mumame [18], an earlier mutation -mapping approach using Usearch [19], which we 86 \ncompare our tool against in a benchmark generated from synthetic reads containing either known 87 \nwildtype or resistance-causing mutations at different read lengths and error rates. 88 \nMaterials and Methods 89 \nReference targets and databases 90 \nAll resistance-determining regions (RDRs) were extracted from the AMRFinderPlus [2] catalogue 91 \n(genes with known SNPs or proteins with known substitutions) and stored as nucleotide and 92 \nprotein entries with metadata (gene, accession, position in reference sequence, mutation(s), 93 \nantimicrobial class) (accessed 17th of July 2025). 94 \nAlignment strategy 95 \nFASTQ reads were six-frame translated and aligned with DIAMOND blastx v 2.1.15 [20] using the 96 \nflags “-F 15 --identity <70-85> --range-culling -k1 --range-cover 5 --iterate --masking 0 --outfmt 6 97 \nqseqid sseqid pident length mismatch gapopen qstart qend sstart send evalue bitscore full_sseq 98 \nqseq_translated” followed by precise alignment and indexing every position using pwalign [21] 99 \nwith the following parameters: “BLOSUM62; gap open −10, gap extend −0.5; local–global scoring”. 100 \nReads were also aligned to RDR DNA sequences with KMA  1.4.15 [22] using the following 101 \ncommand line parameters “-bcNano -hmm -ont -nc -na -1t1 -mrs <0.5-0.85>”; followed by precise 102 \nalignment using pwalign [21] with the following parameters: “match = 2, mismatch = -5, baseOnly 103 \n= FALSE, gapOpening = -10, gapExtension = -0.5, local–global scoring”. 104 \nVariant calling and classification 105 \nFrom the alignments, MetaPointFinder extracts nucleotides or amino acids at canonical positions 106 \nand classifies each read as resistant or wildtype using the curated mutation table obtained from 107 \nAMRFinderPlus [2]. The tool then lists and counts the ARMs and non-ARMs detected per read per 108 \nclass; to note, a read can have multiple ARMs and multiple RDRs for different classes. Ambiguities, 109 \nsuch as non-covered regions, insertions or deletions (indels), unreported substitutions/mutations 110 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n4 \n \nand unknown amino acids (X), are detected and scored as “unknown”. Resistant, wild -type and 111 \nunknown mutations are reported per read, per locus and per antibiotic class. 112 \nSynthetic benchmark design 113 \nTo enable head-to-head comparisons with Mumame, the only known previously published read-114 \ncentric AMR metagenomic analysis tool, we first randomly selected three known mutations from 115 \nthe MetaPointFinder database from each protein and gene in the database and generated protein 116 \nand gene sequences with and without the mutations. Following, we reverse -translated the 117 \nprotein references using EMBOSS backtranseq (https://emboss.sourceforge.net/ v6.6.0) with the 118 \nE. coli codon usage table to DNA sequences. Random nucleotides were padded on both ends of 119 \nthe inferred DNA sequences up to 6 kb and reads where simulated from these 6 kb fragments 120 \nusing wgsim 0.3.1-r13 (https://github.com/lh3/wgsim) at read lengths 100, 200, 1000, and 5000 121 \nbp with error rates between 0 and 30% of which 15% of polymorphisms are indels. Between 1.0 122 \n× 10⁸ and 1.0 × 10⁹ bases of sequence data were generated to ensure that approximately 2.0 × 123 \n10⁴ reads contained an RDR per FASTQ file, with roughly 1.0 × 10⁴  reads representing the wild -124 \ntype variant and 1.0 × 10⁴  reads representing the mutated variant . For DNA benchmarks, 125 \nnucleotide sequences were padded up to 6 kb with random sequences and processed as 126 \ndescribed. The benchmark repository and scripts are available here: 127 \nhttps://github.com/aldertzomer/metapointfinder/tree/main/benchmark and can be rerun from 128 \na functional MetaPointFinder installation. Outputs of the benchmark code include the numbers 129 \nof resistant and wild-type genes detected, and correctly and incorrectly mapped reads per gene. 130 \nMetaPointFinder was run with the following settings “ --identity 50” for the benchmarks. 131 \nMumame database indexing was performed using “mumame_build -m snps.txt -i 132 \nprotein_fasta_protein_variant_model.fasta” which relies on the latest CARD database (v4.0.1) [3] 133 \nand provides the necessary files and the analysis was run on the synthetic benchmark FASTQ files 134 \nusing default settings. 135 \nReal-data re-analyses 136 \nTo evaluate the performance of MetaPointFinder against real-world datasets, we re-analysed five 137 \ndatasets: (i) An Illumina sequencing dataset associated with prolonged selection for resistance 138 \nafter antimicrobial treatment of chickens, focusing on fluoroquinolone and β-lactam resistance 139 \nsignals where genotypic–phenotypic discrepancies have been described [23], obtained from ENA 140 \nSRA PRJEB73721. (ii) An Illumina sequencing dataset of tuberculosis (TB) patients treated with 141 \nrifampicin [24], ENA SRA PRJNA1002577. (iii) An Illumina sequencing dataset of sewage samples 142 \nby Munk et all [12] (see Supplemental Data 1 for all accessions, only read files with more than 5 143 \ngbase of sequencing data were included). (iv) A gyrA/parC amplicon sequencing dataset where no 144 \ndifference was observed between the encoded GyrA/ParC resistance substitutions in Swedish and 145 \nIndian wastewater treatment plants (WWTPs), now analysed without discarding a majority of 146 \nreads [25], ENA SRA PRJNA239415. (v) The abundance of ARGs and ARMs from a set of 1228 long-147 \nread metagenomes obtained from SRA with more than 1 gbase of sequence data per readfile. In 148 \nall cases, reads were downloaded from SRA and analysed without any pre-processing. Resistance 149 \nfractions were determined by dividing resistant RDR counts by the sum of the wildtype and 150 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n5 \n \nresistant RDR counts. Relative RDR abundances were determined by dividing the number of 151 \nresistant RDR counts by the total read counts in file, then multiplying by 1,000,000 to scale the 152 \nrelative abundance to 1,000,000 reads. Heatmaps were generated from RDR fractions or scaled 153 \nrelative abundances using pheatmap [26] using Euclidian distance clustering. RDRs with less than 154 \n10 reads mapped were excluded  from the analysis . Barcharts were generated in Excel or 155 \nGraphPad Prism v10 with whiskers displaying 95% confidence intervals. 156 \nStatistical testing 157 \nRDR counts or percentages were compared using a t-test or with a repeated measurements 158 \nANOVA with Benjamini Hochberg correction for multiple testing in GraphPad Prism v10. 159 \nSignificance (p<0.05) is depicted by “*” in the graphs. Correlation was performed using by 160 \nPearson correlation on (sequencing depth adjusted) readcounts,  log(x + 1) transformed 161 \nreadcounts or by Spearman Rank correlation on sequencing depth adjusted read counts with 162 \nand without zero counts excluded to avoid spurious correlation results.  163 \nImplementation 164 \nMetaPointFinder v1 is implemented in Python 3  (v.3.11.9 was used) and utilises DIAMOND and 165 \nKMA for detection of RDR -containing reads and performs precise alignments in R v4.4.0 using 166 \npwalign v1.2 in Bioconductor 3.20 and Parallel for multiprocessing speedup. It detects ARM 167 \ncontaining reads, parses alignments for precise determination and outputs tabular summaries 168 \n(*.class.prot.summary.txt; *.class.dna.summary.txt)  using Biostrings  2.74.1 from Bioconductor 169 \n3.20. The pipeline accepts (gzipped) FASTQ (short - or long-read) and is species-agnostic. Code is 170 \navailable here: https://github.com/aldertzomer/metapointfinder. Conda and Docker packages 171 \nare available. A web-based implementation is available here: https://klif.uu.nl/metapointfinder/. 172 \nResults 173 \nSimulated benchmark performance 174 \nGold standard non-simulated sequencing datasets are not available for ARM data, therefore we 175 \nsimulated FASTQ-formatted read files from the MetaPointFinder database which contains 260 176 \nproteins and 25 DNA regions. Approximately 20,000 reads out of 20,000 – 1,000,000 reads 177 \n(number of reads depends on the simulated read length) contain an actual RDR per FASTQ file, 178 \nwith approximately 10,000 reads containing a wildtype variant and 10,000 reads containing a 179 \nmutated variant. Reads were both generated from RDRs with amino-acid substitutions and from 180 \nRDRs with DNA mutations, although the latter could not be tested by Mumame as all attempts to 181 \nrun Mumame using a DNA mutation database failed. 182 \nOn all simulated datasets, MetaPointFinder recovered 95-100% of  all known resistant and 183 \nwildtype RDRs up to an introduced error rate of 0.2 (Fig 1A, blue lines). Mumame detected only 184 \n40-55% of the RDRs (Figure 1B, blue lines) at error rates of 0 – 0.03 and then the detection of RDRs 185 \ndrops. A lower error rate (10-1000 fold lower) was observed for MetaPointFinder when comparing 186 \nwith Mumame (Figure 1D vs 1E, orange lines). MetaPointFinder was less sensitive to sequence 187 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n6 \n \ndivergence caused by higher error rates: it still detected genes and mutations with error rates up 188 \nto 30% (Fig 1A vs 1B). However, the number of false positives increased as well, likely caused by 189 \nchanges to the actual ARM (at an error rate of 0.3, almost 1 in 3 bases is changed from the 190 \nreference). Practically no RDRs were detected with Mumame at error rates of 0.2 or higher and 191 \nas a consequence the number of false positives was also 0 (Fig 1B, 1E). 192 \n 193 \nFigure 1. Benchmark data from synthetic read data generated from proteins and genes with known wild -type or 194 \nresistance mutations for various read lengths and error rates causing divergence from actual sequence (0 -0.3 = 0% -195 \n30%). Connected lines: Data from reads originating from wildtype RDRs (WT). Dotted lines: Data from reads originating 196 \nfrom resistant RDRs (RES). In blue shades: correctly predicted. In orange -red shades: false positive (FP: wildtype reads 197 \npredicted as resistant). A: MetaPointFinder amino -acid performance. B: MUMAME amino -acid performance C: 198 \nMetaPointFinder DNA performance. D: MetaPointFinder RDR amino-acid performance. E: MUMAME RDR amino-acid 199 \nperformance. Row F: MetaPointFinder RDR DNA performance. 200 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n7 \n \nIllumina fluoroquinolone and beta-lactam resistome data  201 \nTo evaluate the effectiveness of MetaPointFinder on Illumina sequencing data, we investigated a 202 \nlongitudinally sampled dataset from Swinkels et al. [23] of chickens treated with enrofloxacin, 203 \namoxicillin and doxycycline. Here, phenotypic fluoroquinolone resistance in isolated E. coli from 204 \nsamples was clearly observed after enrofloxacin treatment for all timepoints, with all isolates 205 \nshowing resistance to enrofloxacin and carrying resistance causing mutations in the gene coding 206 \nfor GyrA. The authors reported that Illumina metagenomic analysis of the faecal samples did not 207 \nshow an appreciable increase in the fluoroquinolone resistome; the metagenomes were searched 208 \nfor acquired ARGs. We analysed the Illumina data and show that a statistically significant relative 209 \nincrease in fluoroquinolone ARMs could be observed in the enrofloxacin treated flocks (Figure 210 \n2A). A statistically significant relative increase in beta -lactam ARMs was also observed in the 211 \namoxycillin treated flocks (figure 2B) in timepoint 1, similar as what was observed for the 212 \nphenotypic and ARG resistome data in the original study. No increase was observed for ARMs for 213 \ntetracyclines as few ARMs were detected (not shown). Interestingly, the relative increase in 214 \nquinolone resistance seems to be mostly caused by disappearance of the strains not carrying a 215 \nresistance causing mutation (Figure 2C) for the enrofloxacin treated flocks. 216 \n 217 \nFigure 2. Percentage resistance and read counts of reads predicted to carry a wildtype base(s) or resistance mutation, 218 \nbased on re-analysis of the metagenomics sequence data from Swinkels et al [23] . Left (AC): Quinolone resistance in 219 \nenrofloxacin treated flocks vs controls. Right (BD): Beta-lactam resistance in amoxicillin treated flocks. Top (AB). Y-220 \naxis: % of reads predicted to carry a resistance mutation. X-axis: timepoint. Bottom (BD): Y-axis: number of reads 221 \npredicted to carry a wildtype or resistance mutation. X-axis: timepoint. Significant differences are marked by * 222 \n(p<0.05, t-test). 223 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n8 \n \nIllumina rifamycin resistome data 224 \nTo evaluate the performance of MetaPointFinder on detecting RpoB amino acid substitutions in 225 \nmetagenomes, we re-analysed sequence data of Diallo et al [24]. Here, the gut microbiome of 226 \ntuberculosis (TB) patients was sequenced using Illumina sequencing at timepoint 0 (before 227 \ntreatment), at 2 and 6 months (during treatment with rifampicin) and at 15 months (9 months 228 \nafter treatment). Exposure to rifampicin generally results in mutations in RpoB in many bacterial 229 \nspecies [27]. In the original study, the authors showed that the gut microbiome of TB patients 230 \nduring changes during treatment and the altered microbial community in the gut environment 231 \npersisted for at least 9 months after treatment completion. The authors did not discuss the 232 \nresistome for us to make comparisons with. Using MetaPointFinder, we show that resistance to 233 \nrifamycins was higher in treated patients during treatment, but that the rifamycin resistance in 234 \nthe gut microbiome after treatment was no longer different to the before treatment sample 235 \n(Figure 3). RpoB mutations come with a fitness cost and without antimicrobial pressure, 236 \nresistant strains are lost from the microbiome [27] .  237 \n0 2 6 15\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\ntimepoint (months)\n% reads resistant to rifamycin\n✱\n✱\n 238 \nFigure 3 Percentage of reads mapped to rifamycin RDRs resistant to rifamycin as compared to wildtype reads in 4 239 \ntimepoints from Diallo et al [24]. In orange: during treatment. In blue, before or after treatment. 240 \nIllumina resistome data for surveillance purposes 241 \nTo evaluate the use of MetaPointFinder for surveillance purposes, we selected and downloaded 242 \nall Illumina read files from a study by Munk et al [12] describing the use of metagenomics for 243 \nAMR surveillance of sewage from 101 countries from all continents (Figure 4). Sequencing data 244 \nwere processed using MetaPointFinder and the resistant percentage of RDRs and relative 245 \nabundance of resistant RDR were clustered and displayed as heatmaps. Similar to Munk et al, 246 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n9 \n \nclustering on RDR data by continent is observed and some resistant RDRs are mainly found in 247 \nEurope (Figure 4AB). Completely analysing the Munk et al dataset is outside the scope of this 248 \nmanuscript, but for instance the fraction of resistant pleuromutilin RDR 249 \nABC−F_type_ribosomal_protection_protein_Eat(A) is much higher in Europe than in Asia, LAC or 250 \nAfrica which is visible in the percentage analysis. High pleuromutilin resistance has been noted 251 \nbefore in Europe because of the use of tiamulin in farm animals [28].  252 \n 253 \n 254 \nFigure 4. Heatmap of resistance of RDRs in sewage data from 101 countries. Clustered ARM heatmap showing the 255 \npercentage resistance (A) and relative abundance (B) of the 25 most variable ARMs in the dataset from Munk et al 256 \n[12]. LAC: Latin America and the Caribbean.  257 \nComparison of ARGs and ARMs in Nanopore sequencing data 258 \nNo long-read benchmark metagenomics sequencing dataset is available with known quantities of 259 \nresistance based on phenotypic antimicrobial resistance data . Based on the assumption that 260 \nresistance caused by  both ARGs and ARMs are a result of selection caused by exposure to 261 \nantibiotics, we could assume that samples with high counts of ARGs may also have a high count 262 \nof ARMs. To evaluate the performance of MetaPointFinder on Nanopore data, we downloaded all 263 \nlong-read metagenomes from the SRA with more than 1 gigabase per sample (n=1228) and we 264 \ndetermined ARG counts per class by aligning all reads with the AMRFinderPlus protein database. 265 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n10 \n \nThe ARM counts were determined using MetaPointFinder with a sequence identity cutoff 70% to 266 \naccommodate the higher expected error rates of long-read metagenomic datasets. The ARG and 267 \nARM readcounts were corrected for total read number.  Spearman Rank and log+1 transformed 268 \nPearson correlation between ARG and ARM per sample was determined per class (Supplemental 269 \nData 1, Table 1). For many classes, a correlation was observed between ARG and ARM abundance. 270 \nFor colistin and macrolide drug classes, the observed correlation was low or absent, possibly 271 \nbecause the number of samples with more than 10 reads with ARGs was low for colistin (35 272 \nsamples) and the number of samples with ARM reads was low for macrolides (79 samples). 273 \nTable 1. Pearson and Spearman Rank Correlation of ARG and ARM abundance corrected for read depth 274 \nClass Pearson Pearson* \nLog(x+1) \nPearson Spearman Spearman* \naminoglycoside 0.14 0.19 0.32 0.32 0.33 \nbeta-lactam 0.44 0.46 0.48 0.43 0.43 \ncolistin 0.02 -0.01 0.19 0.21 0.42 \nfosfomycin 0.25 0.38 0.64 0.57 0.65 \nfusidic acid 0.42 0.52 0.51 0.34 0.60 \nmacrolide 0.00 0.04 0.18 0.23 0.10 \nquinolone 0.17 0.37 0.4 0.33 0.61 \nrifamycin 0.13 0.26 0.33 0.28 0.49 \ntetracycline 0.17 0.28 0.13 0.13 0.10 \n* Samples with zero counts have been excluded. All r values above 0.056 are significant.  275 \nAnalysis of Nanopore deep sequencing data 276 \nIn Bloemen et al. [29], a study to evaluate the ability of long -read sequencing to detect very low 277 \nnumbers of fluoroquinolone point mutations in deep sequenced data using haplotype -aware 278 \nassembly methods, phasing of gyrase mutations from the metagenomics assemblies was 279 \nchallenging. Here, MetaPointFinder detected RDR substitutions directly in long reads. Both 280 \nresistant and wildtype reads were detected, albeit lower in Sample A where detection completely 281 \nfailed in the original study (Table 2). 282 \nTable 2. Fluoroquinolone RDR counts of two deep metagenomic samples sequenced. WT=wildtype, RES=resistant.  283 \nRDR Sample A Sample B \nWT RES WT RES \nDNA gyrase subunit A GyrA 72 4 430 28 \nDNA topoisomerase (ATP-hydrolyzing) subunit B GyrB 138 2 148 4 \nDNA topoisomerase IV subunit A ParC 98 8 222 52 \nDNA topoisomerase IV subunit B ParE 104 8 126 2 \nAmplicon datasets  284 \nApplying MetaPointFinder to a gyrA/parC amplicon sequencing study  by Johnning et al  [25], 285 \nexamining fluoroquinolone resistance in Swedish and Indian river water samples  taken from 286 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n11 \n \ndifferent distances from a WWTP , revealed location-specific differences in gyrA/parC mutation 287 \nburdens that were obscured by discarding a large fraction of reads in the workflow used by the 288 \nauthors of the original study (Table 4). Twice as many reads are mapped by MetaPointFinder than 289 \nthe original study’s custom workflow and a clear difference was observed in upstream samples 290 \n(80 % vs 63%, Table 3). Swedish and Indian river water samples up- and downstream of a waste-291 \nwater treatment plant contain different levels of ARMs, although statistical testing cannot be 292 \nperformed as there are no  replicates. Surprisingly, the amplicon reads also encode hits to GyrB 293 \nand ParE, likely because of the conservation of the genes encoding these proteins results in off -294 \ntarget amplification and subsequent identification by MetaPointFinder. 295 \nTable 3. Reads mapped to quinolone RDRs and percentage of RDRs resistant of total wildtype and resistant RDRs 296 \nSample location with \nregards to WWTP \nTotal \nRead \ncount \nreads \nmapped \nJohnning \net al \nreads mapped \nMetaPointFinder \n \nGyrA \n%resistant \nGyrB \n%resistant \nParC \n%resistant \nParE \n%resistant \nIndian 1.9 km \nupstream 31384 3762 18893 80% 1% 4% 5% \nIndian 2.3 km \ndownstream 30094 2939 14644 75% 1% 65% 6% \nIndian 2.7 Km \ndownstream 27483 4215 12695 62% 2% 38% 2% \nIndian 17.5 km \ndownstream 26277 2262 12180 57% 2% 0% 2% \nSwedish 5-100 m \nupstream 29010 4885 16412 63% 4% 23% 3% \nSwedish 25-230 m \ndownstream 26035 2719 13682 59% 6% 13% 3% \nDiscussion 297 \nMetaPointFinder addresses a key gap in metagenomic resistome profiling , the identification of 298 \nmutation-driven AMR, by directly querying known resistance-conferring substitutions at the read 299 \nlevel. Compared with prior mutation mappers such as Mumame, our translated amino -acid and 300 \nDNA alignment strategy improves recovery and quantification, especially for long reads or 301 \nsequences with higher divergence from the reference sequences. Mumame relies on the free 32 302 \nbit version of USEARCH which is limited to files with a maximum of 4 gbytes [30], thereby limiting 303 \nits use cases. We show that MetaPointFinder detects twice as much RDRs with 10-1000 fold less 304 \nfalse positives as compared to Mumame, depending on read length and error rate.  305 \nWhile AMRFinderPlus and RGI catalogue known SNPs effectively on assembled isolate genomes, 306 \nand RGI command-line can process metagenomes, these tools are not primarily designed for per-307 \nread ARM quantification of metagenomic data as species have to be selected a priori. Secondly, 308 \nthe difficulty with assembled metagenomes is that minor variant ARMs are lost in standard 309 \nassemblies. In Bloemen et al [29], an attempt was made to use haplotype -aware assembly 310 \nmethods on two metagenomic samples, sequenced at a high depth, to phase out genomes from 311 \nthe same species, but this resulted in limited success. The authors only detected the gyrase 312 \nmutation in one sample. In many cases, read-based analysis, like commonly used for ARGs, is the 313 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n12 \n \npreferred method for ARMs as well as it allows for the identification of ARMs in metagenomes 314 \nthat might be variably present  in different members of the same species . It  does not rely on 315 \nassembly, which requires higher read depths of both wildtype and resistant RDR as compared to 316 \nread-based analysis. 317 \nBecause MetaPointFinder not only scores resistant RDRs but also explicitly quantifies wild -type 318 \nRDRs, the resistan t proportion can be determined, similar to the proportion -based indicator -319 \norganism methods used in phenotypic surveillance; in both approaches, resistance is expressed 320 \nas a fraction of the total population , resistant reads in metagenomes or resistant colonies in 321 \nculture, reflecting the same underlying surveillance philosophy described by e.g. Dorado-García 322 \net al and EFSA/ECDC guidelines  [31, 32] . In addition, MetaPointFinder can express mutation -323 \ndriven resistance in terms of relative abundance within the metagenomic read pool, analogous to 324 \nhow ARG abundances are routinely quantified in resistome profiling. This relative abundance 325 \nreflects the load of resistance and is important for risk assessment, as higher abundance is directly 326 \nassociated with increased risk of resistance dissemination [33–35]. 327 \nLimitations of MetaPointFinder include reliance on known mutations and a single database source 328 \n(AMRFinderPlus). Future improvements could include including more known ARM data from 329 \npublished sources. Another improvement could be the assessment of mutations from multiple 330 \ndatabase hits for a single RDR, thereby increasing sensitivity, possibly at the cost of specificity. A 331 \nfurther improvement would be to include an extended, non -evidence based experimental 332 \ndatabase by also scoring conservative alternative substitutions informed by substitution matrices 333 \nand probabilistic scoring, or machine learning -based predicting of similar mutations, or protein 334 \nstructure informed alternative substitutions. However, these are computer predictions that need 335 \nto be used with caution until validated in vitro. We plan to release a predicted resistance mutation 336 \ndatabase addressing these potential improvements. A final limitation to using read based analysis 337 \nis that resistances that require a combination of mutations  in several genes , such as penicillin 338 \nresistance in S. pneumoniae [36] cannot be evaluated as the presence of the combination of 339 \nmutations in a single phased out genome sequence from a metagenome cannot yet be reliably 340 \ndetermined. 341 \nConclusion 342 \nMetaPointFinder enables robust detection and quantification of known resistance -conferring 343 \npoint mutations directly from metagenomic reads, complementing gene -centric analyses and 344 \nimproving surveillance of mutation-driven AMR, especially for classes of resistance that are mostly 345 \ndriven by ARMs instead of ARGs. 346 \nData and Code Availability 347 \nMetaPointFinder repository https://github.com/aldertzomer/metapointfinder . Benchmark 348 \nscripts: https://github.com/aldertzomer/metapointfinder/benchmark . Online version 349 \n.CC-BY 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted December 5, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.64898/2025.12.04.691125doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n13 \n \nhttps://klif.uu.nl/metapointfinder/ . Supplemental data 1 used to generate tables in figures in 350 \nthis manuscript: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17737473 351 \nAcknowledgments 352 \nAZ and SN acknowledge funding from the Netherlands Organisation for Health Research and 353 \nDevelopment (ZonMw) [Grant 10430022010002]. AEM acknowledges funding from the UK 354 \nResearch and Innovation (UKRI) Medical Research Council (MRC) [Grant MR/Y034457/1], AEM 355 \nand CC acknowledge funding from the Biotechnology and Biological Sciences Research Council 356 \n(BBSRC) Institute Strategic Programme Microbes and Food Safety BB/X011011/1 and its 357 \nconstituent project BBS/E/QU/230002C (Theme 3, Flexible capabilities to reduce food safety 358 \nthreats and respond to national needs). The authors acknowledge support from the Joint 359 \nProgramming Initiative on Antimicrobial Resistance (JPIAMR) through the MEGAISurv project, 360 \nand the BBSRC Europe Partnering Award BB/V01823X/1. The authors also acknowledge the use 361 \nof ChatGPT (version 5; OpenAI) for assistance in code curation and grammar correction. 362 \nReferences 363 \n1. Bortolaia V, Kaas RS, Ruppe E, Roberts MC, Schwarz S, Cattoir V, et al. ResFinder 4.0 for 364 \npredictions of phenotypes from genotypes. J Antimicrob Chemother. 2020;75:3491–500. 365 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/JAC/DKAA345. 366 \n2. Feldgarden M, Brover V, Gonzalez-Escalona N, Frye JG, Haendiges J, Haft DH, et al. 367 \nAMRFinderPlus and the Reference Gene Catalog facilitate examination of the genomic links 368 \namong antimicrobial resistance, stress response, and virulence. Sci Rep. 2021;11. 369 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/S41598-021-91456-0. 370 \n3. Alcock BP, Huynh W, Chalil R, Smith KW, Raphenya AR, Wlodarski MA, et al. CARD 2023: 371 \nexpanded curation, support for machine learning, and resistome prediction at the 372 \nComprehensive Antibiotic Resistance Database. Nucleic Acids Res. 2023;51:D690–9. 373 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/NAR/GKAC920. 374 \n4. Tyson GH, Zhao S, Li C, Ayers S, Sabo JL, Lam C, et al. Establishing Genotypic Cutoff Values To 375 \nMeasure Antimicrobial Resistance in Salmonella. Antimicrob Agents Chemother. 2017;61. 376 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1128/AAC.02140-16. 377 \n5. Tyson GH, McDermott PF, Li C, Chen Y, Tadesse DA, Mukherjee S, et al. WGS accurately 378 \npredicts antimicrobial resistance in Escherichia coli. J Antimicrob Chemother. 2015;70:2763–9. 379 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/jac/dkv186. 380 \n6. Biffignandi GB, Chindelevitch L, Corbella M, Feil EJ, Sassera D, Lees JA. Optimising machine 381 \nlearning prediction of minimum inhibitory concentrations in Klebsiella pneumoniae. Microb 382 \nGenomics. 2024;10:001222. https://doi.org/10.1099/MGEN.0.001222. 383 \n7. Mobegi FM, Cremers AJH, De Jonge MI, Bentley SD, Van Hijum SAFT, Zomer A. Deciphering 384 \nthe distance to antibiotic resistance for the pneumococcus using genome sequencing data. Sci 385 \nRep. 2017;7:42808. https://doi.org