AJ, Zomer AL, Wagenaar JA. Extended period of 428 \nselection for antimicrobial resistance due to recirculation of persistent antimicrobials in broilers. 429 \nJ Antimicrob Chemother. 2024;79:2186–93. https://doi.org/10.1093/jac/dkae213. 430 \n24. Diallo D, Sun S, Somboro AM, Baya B, Koné A, Diarra B, et al. Metabolic and immune 431 \nconsequences of antibiotic related microbiome alterations during first-line tuberculosis 432 \ntreatment in Bamako, Mali. Front Immunol. 2025;16:1561459. 433 \nhttps://doi.org/10.3389/FIMMU.2025.1561459. 434 \n25. Johnning A, Kristiansson E, Fick J, Weijdegård B, Larsson DGJ. Resistance mutations in gyrA 435 \nand parC are common in Escherichia communities of both fluoroquinolone-polluted and 436 \nuncontaminated aquatic environments. Front Microbiol. 2015;6 DEC:160210. 437 \nhttps://doi.org/10.3389/FMICB.2015.01355. 438 \n26. Kolde R. Pretty Heatmaps [R package pheatmap version 1.0.13]. CRAN Contrib Packag. 2025. 439 \nhttps://doi.org/10.32614/CRAN.PACKAGE.PHEATMAP. 440 \n27. Bolourchi N, Brown CRP, Letten AD, Engelstädter J. Evolution and evolvability of rifampicin 441 \nresistance across the bacterial tree of life. Proc Natl Acad Sci. 2025;122:e2424307122. 442 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1073/PNAS.2424307122. 443 \n28. Van Duijkeren E, Greko C, Pringle M, Baptiste KE, Catry B, Jukes H, et al. Pleuromutilins: use 444 \nin food-producing animals in the European Union, development of resistance and impact on 445 \nhuman and animal health. J Antimicrob Chemother. 2014;69:2022–31. 446 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/JAC/DKU123. 447 \n29. Bloemen B, Gand M, Ringenier M, Bogaerts B, Vanneste K, Marchal K, et al. Overcoming 448 \nchallenges in metagenomic AMR surveillance with nanopore sequencing: a case study on 449 \nfluoroquinolone resistance. Front Microbiol. 2025;16:1614301. 450 \nhttps://doi.org/10.3389/FMICB.2025.1614301. 451 \n30. Zhou Y, Liu YX, Li X. USEARCH 12: Open-source software for sequencing analysis in 452 \nbioinformatics and microbiom