input 821 \nsequences, PPLM is used to generate the embeddings, intra-protein attention matrices and inter-protein attention 822 \nmatrix, which are aggregated using max and mean pooling. The aggregated features are concatenated and passed 823 \nthrough a multilayer perceptron to predict the interaction probability between the input sequences. (C) PPLM-824 \nAffinity pipeline. The final transformer block of PPLM is finetuned on binding affinity data, where the resulting 825 \nembeddings for paired sequences are aggregated via max pooling and passed through two fully connected layers 826 \nto predict binding affinity value. (D) PPLM-Contact pipeline. For a given sequence pairs, inter-protein attention 827 \nmatrix from PPLM is integrated with MSA -derived features and monomer distance maps to capture both 828 \nevolutionary and structural information. These features are processed by a series of inter -protein transformer 829 \nblocks, with each composed of three core modules to predict the inter-protein contacts. Each core module adopts 830 \na parallel architecture to update inter-protein representations using both intra- and inter-protein features.  831 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licensemade available under a \n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is \nThe copyright holder for this preprintthis version posted July 10, 2025. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.07.663595doi: bioRxiv preprint \n\n 20 \n 832 \nFigure 2. Perplexity of PPLM and ESM2 on protein sequence pairs. (A) Perplexity distribution for 833 \nsequence pairs from the  PDB and STRING databases under random masking and interface masking 834 \nstrategies. Interface masking is applied only to sequence pairs from PDB database, with single-chain 835 \ninterface masking used exclusively for heterodimers . (B) Head-to-head comparison of perplexity  836 \nbetween PPLM and ESM2  under random masking . (C) H